[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Secret Management and Sensitive Data Handling in Terraform"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Managing secrets securely is one of the most critical aspects of Infrastructure as Code (IaC). Terraform configuration files, state files, and execution plans often interact with sensitive data such as database passwords, API tokens, and private keys. Failing to handle these secrets correctly can lead to severe security breaches."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Risks of Plaintext Secrets"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you define variables in Terraform, it is tempting to pass sensitive values directly in your terraform.tfvars file or hardcode them into your configuration. However, Terraform stores state in a JSON file that is often saved in plaintext (unless remote encryption is properly configured). Anyone with access to the state file can read plaintext secrets."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the sensitive Argument"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Terraform allows you to mark input variables, output values, and locals as sensitive. This prevents Terraform from displaying the values in the command-line output during execution plans, reducing the risk of accidental exposure in CI/CD logs."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"variable "db_password" {
  type        = string
  description = "Database administrator password"
  sensitive   = true
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating with Secret Managers"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The industry best practice for handling sensitive data in Terraform is to avoid storing secrets in the repository or local variables altogether. Instead, use dedicated secret management services like AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, Azure Key Vault, or Google Cloud Secret Manager, and fetch them dynamically during the Terraform run."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"data "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "db_secret" {
  secret_id = "prod/database/password"
}

resource "aws_db_instance" "example" {
  identifier = "my-rds-db"
  password   = data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.db_secret.secret_string
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Secret Management"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never commit terraform.tfvars or terraform.tfstate files to version control systems like GitHub."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enable encryption at rest for your remote state backends (e.g., AWS S3 bucket encryption with KMS)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit access permissions to state files and secret management stores strictly to authorized personnel and CI/CD pipelines."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly rotate secrets stored in your secret manager to minimize the impact of potential leaks."}]
